# A cusp form with vanishing period integrals is zero

Theorem statement: `MTT.period_vanishing` (`12542542-b0fc-4959-b734-052b70cdacbc`), by allychan327,
2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `292b4137-5c7a-4f34-afbc-8762c85a5d15`, by allychan327, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

If every modular integral of a weight-k cusp form against X^j vanishes, for all degrees j from 0 to
k-2 and every rational base point r, then the cusp form is zero. Equivalently, the period integrals
along the vertical rays from the rational cusps to i-infinity, taken with all polynomial weights of
degree at most k-2, separate cusp forms. This is the injectivity half of the Eichler-Shimura
correspondence. It is not a formal consequence of Hecke equivariance or of multiplicity one, both of
which constrain the image of the period map rather than its kernel.

Formalization note. Expanding the q-expansion under the integral is not by itself a proof. On a
vertical ray truncated at height T > 0 the interchange is legitimate and produces the series with
n-th term a_n e(nr) e^{-2 pi n T} / (2 pi n), but letting T tend to 0 is an Abel limit rather than a
convergent sum: the Hecke bound only gives a_n = O(n^{k/2}), so the coefficients a_n / n need not be
summable, and the resulting function of r is not continuous. A proof has to control the period map
itself, not merely its formal Fourier expansion.

## Explanation of the source proof

`period_vanishing` is the degenerate case of Eichler–Shimura injectivity, and needs nothing
beyond it.

Suppose every vertical period of $f$ vanishes: $\int_0^\infty f(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt = 0$ for all
$r\in\mathbf Q$ and all $0\le j\le k-2$. The period polynomial based at a rational cusp is by
definition
$$F_f(r) \;=\; \sum_{j=0}^{k-2}\binom{k-2}{j}\Big(2\pi\!\int_0^\infty\! f(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt\Big)
  X^jY^{k-2-j},$$
so every one of its coefficients is a listed period, and $F_f \equiv 0$ on the rational cusps;
at $\infty$ it vanishes by definition (`prim_eq_zero`). The same computation applied to the zero
form gives $F_0 \equiv 0$ — worth stating separately only because `modularIntegral` is a total
function defined by a Lebesgue integral, so $F_0 = 0$ is the statement that the integral of the
zero integrand vanishes rather than a syntactic identity (`modularIntegral_zero`).

Now apply `period_cocycle_injective` with the holomorphic part $g = f$, the antiholomorphic part
$h = 0$ and the coboundary datum $P = 0$: its hypothesis asks that the period cocycle
$$\gamma \;\longmapsto\; F_g(\gamma\infty) + \epsilon\cdot F_h(\epsilon\,\gamma\infty)$$
be the coboundary $\gamma\cdot P - P$, and with these choices both sides are identically $0$. The
conclusion $g = h = 0$ gives $f = 0$.

So the content of `period_vanishing` is exactly the injectivity half of Eichler–Shimura,
specialized to the case where the coboundary is trivial. Nothing analytic is added here: the
vanishing hypothesis is strong enough that the cocycle is not merely cohomologous to zero but
literally zero.

